# Allowed license options for the Python dependency projections
# (apps/api, apps/worker, apps/mcp-server).
#
# One option per line. Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored; matching is
# case-insensitive.
#
# Three kinds of entry appear here, read from three different metadata sources:
#
#   * SPDX identifiers -- what a PEP 639 `License-Expression` declares in the
#     core metadata, and the normal case for a modern wheel (the probe in
#     scripts/dependency_governance.py reads that field first);
#   * the free-text strings the probe reports for packages that still only
#     publish a legacy `License` field or a Trove license classifier
#     ("BSD License", "Apache Software License", "GNU General Public
#     License (GPL)", ...). Free text is not authoritative metadata, so the
#     projections never guess: a package that declares none of the three
#     sources stays `UNKNOWN` and keeps failing closed;
#   * a `LicenseRef-*` identifier, which is only ever an entry here when the
#     policy explicitly accepts that non-standard reference -- it is never
#     matched by name similarity to another license.
#
# Compound expressions (`A AND B`, `A OR B`, `A WITH B`) are split into their
# options by scripts/dependency_governance.py before this list is applied, so
# add atoms here, never expressions.
#
# Every addition is a reviewable policy decision: it whitelists a license for
# everything built from these three dependency graphs.
0BSD
Apache License 2.0
Apache Software License
Apache-2.0
BSD
BSD License
BSD-2-Clause
BSD-3-Clause
CC0-1.0
GNU General Public License (GPL)
GNU General Public License v3 or later (GPLv3+)
GNU Lesser General Public License v3 (LGPLv3)
GNU Library or Lesser General Public License (LGPL)
ISC
ISC License
ISC License (ISCL)
LicenseRef-Biopython-License-Agreement
MIT
MIT License
MIT-0
MIT-CMU
MPL-2.0
Mozilla Public License 2.0 (MPL 2.0)
PSF-2.0
Python Software Foundation License
The Unlicense (Unlicense)
Zlib

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Declared-unknown fixups
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A package that publishes none of the three metadata sources is `UNKNOWN` and
# fails closed -- that rule is what makes the gate meaningful, so it is not
# relaxed for a whole graph. A small number of transitive dependencies,
# however, ship a wheel that genuinely omits the license metadata even though
# the project's own repository declares an allowed license:
#
#   * google-crc32c 1.8.0 -- no `License-Expression`, no `License`, no
#     license classifier in the wheel; upstream is Apache-2.0
#     (https://github.com/googleapis/python-crc32c/blob/main/LICENSE).
#   * scverse-misc 0.1.3 -- only pastes the BSD-3-Clause text into the legacy
#     `License` field, which is a license *text* rather than a machine-readable
#     declaration.
#
# The syntax is `@allow-undeclared <name>=<license>`: the named package is
# accepted only when its declaration is `UNKNOWN`, and only with the license
# stated here. It is per-package and per-license, so it can never blanket a
# graph, and it is a reviewable policy decision like every other line in this
# file -- the license must be in the allow-list above.
#
# Do not add an entry without a link to the upstream license in the commit.
@allow-undeclared google-crc32c=Apache-2.0
@allow-undeclared scverse-misc=BSD-3-Clause
